INT. ABANDONED CARNIVAL FUNHOUSE - NIGHT

Cracked mirrors. Painted clown faces peeling off plywood. A rusted carousel horse hangs from a wire. Wind whistles through broken boards.

Three friends pick through the dark with flashlights.

MARISOL "MARI" ENGELBRECHT-OJO (20s, hoodie, nervous laugh) leads. She points her beam down a narrow corridor lined with warped glass.

TREVOR KAPOOR-LINDSTROM (20s, camera on a strap, bravado) follows, filming.

WINIFRED "WINNIE" BAPTISTE-HOLLOWAY (20s, backpack, serious) brings up the rear, checking a paper map.

TREVOR
Okay, so, this is the Hall of Mirrors. Day one of the haunted-places series. Say hi, Mari.

MARI
Hi.

TREVOR
Say hi, Winnie.

WINNIE
I'm reading.

TREVOR
Reading what?

WINNIE
The map. It says there's an exit through the carousel.

MARI
Then why are we in the funhouse?

TREVOR
Content.

WINNIE
Idiot.

TREVOR
Be nice. I'm broadcasting.

MARI
(over his shoulder)
Hold on. Trevor.

TREVOR
What?

MARI
There's three of us.

TREVOR
Yes.

MARI
In the mirror there's four.

They stop. Mari points. In a cracked panel, three flashlight beams. And a fourth, steady, a pale light that doesn't match any of them.

WINNIE
It's a reflection.

MARI
Of what?

TREVOR
Whoa, whoa. Everyone breathe.

They turn in a slow circle. The fourth light is gone. The mirror shows only themselves.

TREVOR (CONT'D)
(quiet)
Cool. That was cool.

WINNIE
We leave. Now.

MARI
Yes. Yes. Let's go.

They back down the corridor, shoulder to shoulder. A laugh echoes. Not theirs. Light and high, a child's.

They sprint. Boots clatter. A mirror shatters, then another, one after another, as if a hand were sweeping through.

Trevor drops the camera. It spins on the floor, filming the ceiling. Winnie grabs Mari's wrist. Mari grabs Trevor's collar. They crash through a painted door into the carousel hall.

The carousel horses are all turned to face them.

TREVOR
No.

MARI
Don't move.

WINNIE
Don't look.

The music box starts. Slow, wheezing.

MARI
(whispering)
Winnie, the map. The exit.

WINNIE
Behind the red horse.

MARI
Which is the red horse?

They all look. Twenty horses. Eighteen are white. One is black. One is red.

The red horse blinks.

They scream. In unison. A single voice that rattles the roof.

Then the music stops, as though a hand had pressed it silent.

A voice, close, warm, from just behind the three of them.

CHILD (O.S.)
You came back.

Mari whips around. Nobody there.

MARI
(under her breath)
We've never been here.

CHILD (O.S.)
You came back.

Trevor drops to his knees, scooping the camera. Its red light blinks.

TREVOR
Please. Please. We'll leave. We'll never--

The voice laughs.

CHILD (O.S.)
Say it again.

TREVOR
We'll leave.

CHILD (O.S.)
Say it with love.

WINNIE
(sudden, firm)
No.

She stands. She faces the dark where the voice is. Raises her flashlight, and aims it at the red horse.

WINNIE (CONT'D)
We're not saying anything. We're leaving. You can have the funhouse.

Silence. The wind stops.

Then the red horse, slowly, turns its head away.

WINNIE (CONT'D)
(to the others)
Walk. Don't run.

They walk. Past the horses. Past the dead ticket booth. Past the gate.

Behind them, the music box begins again. Soft. Almost happy.
